 ## check.sh
 
-### §check-fails-open — the check never blocks a commit because of its own failure
-- Since: 2026-09-11
-- Decision: `set -uo pipefail` without `-e`. Every internal error still ends in `exit 0`; the script only warns.
-- Forbidden: `set -e` or `set -o errexit`, and any exit status other than 0.
-- Reason: with `set -e`, a `wc` failure on a staged-then-deleted file aborted the check and blocked every commit in
-  the repository for a day (2026-09-10). The check is advisory by design.
+### §check-fails-closed — a failing command aborts the check with a non-zero exit
+- Since: 2026-10-23 (the maintainer overrode §check-fails-open of 2026-09-11, which forbade `set -e` and any non-zero
+  exit)
+- Decision: `set -euo pipefail`. Any failing command aborts the check with a non-zero exit. Large staged files still
+  only produce a warning, and a clean run ends in `exit 0`.
+- Forbidden: dropping `-e`, or turning internal errors back into warnings, without a new override from the maintainer.
+  Inside the file loop, do not end the body with `cmd && echo`: when the last file is under the limit, the loop
+  returns 1 and the check aborts with no output. Use `if … then … fi`.
+- Reason: the maintainer decided the check must fail when it breaks instead of passing silently. The 2026-09-10
+  incident behind the old rule (a `wc` failure on a staged-then-deleted file blocked every commit for a day) is
+  handled by the `[ -f "$f" ] || continue` guard, which skips files that no longer exist on disk.
